Use a dictionary trie and prefix dynamic programming: reachable[i] means s.slice(0,i) can be segmented. From each reachable position, follow trie edges and mark endpoints of dictionary words. Empty s returns true; empty dictionary entries do not advance segmentation.

```javascript
function solve(s, words) {
  if (s.length === 0) return true;

  const root = { next: new Map(), terminal: false };

  for (const word of words) {
    let node = root;
    for (let i = 0; i < word.length; i++) {
      const ch = word[i];
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) {
        node.next.set(ch, { next: new Map(), terminal: false });
      }
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.terminal = true;
  }

  const n = s.length;
  const reachable = new Array(n + 1).fill(false);
  reachable[0] = true;

  for (let start = 0; start < n; start++) {
    if (!reachable[start]) continue;

    let node = root;
    for (let end = start; end < n; end++) {
      node = node.next.get(s[end]);
      if (node === undefined) break;
      if (node.terminal) reachable[end + 1] = true;
    }
  }

  return reachable[n];
}
```

Call solve(s, words) directly; no imports, exports, or stdin/stdout. With n = s.length, W dictionary entries, and L total dictionary length, worst-case time is O(W + L + n²), and extra space is O(L + n), assuming constant-time Map operations. Empty s takes O(1).

