Use rolling dynamic programming: reaching position i costs the cheaper of paying at i−1 or i−2 and moving to i. Starting at index 1 is free; initialization also allows reaching it through index 0 if that is cheaper, so negative costs work too.

```javascript
function solve(cost) {
  const n = cost.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;

  let twoBack = 0;
  let oneBack = Math.min(0, cost[0]);

  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    const current = Math.min(
      oneBack + cost[i - 1],
      twoBack + cost[i - 2]
    );
    twoBack = oneBack;
    oneBack = current;
  }

  return oneBack;
}
```

Call solve(cost) directly; no imports, exports, or stdin/stdout. Time: O(n). Extra space: O(1).

